{"id": "c19561e0-b908-4404-af31-7e3cc1f356aa", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"id": "tooluse_VTQagICdwO23ivmoVW3zlR", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-quota-review", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics"], "prompt": "{
 "task": "Determine the actual Amazon Bedrock runtime quotas (requests/min and tokens/min) in us-east-1 for account 123456789012, whether they differ from AWS defaults, and which models are actually being used/throttled.",
 "context": "Customer is being throttled on Bedrock in us-east-1 during business hours and says they never requested a quota increase. They want to know their actual limit and what to do. Read-only work only.",
 "resources": ["account 123456789012", "region us-east-1", "service-quotas ServiceCode bedrock", "CloudWatch namespace AWS/Bedrock"],
 "timeframe": "last 14 days up to 2026-11-23T21:00:00Z, with focus on weekday business hours (roughly 13:00-23:00 UTC)",
 "criteria": "1) Page through ALL list_service_quotas results (ServiceCode=bedrock; responses are paginated with NextToken and return small pages, keep following until no token). Keep only RPM/TPM quotas (on-demand, cross-region, global cross-region) relevant to models actually in use. 2) For those quota codes, compare Value to get_aws_default_service_quota; flag where applied != default, and check list_requested_service_quota_change_history_by_service for any past increases (also note any Adjustable=false quotas). 3) Find which models are used: CloudWatch AWS/Bedrock metrics (Invocations, InvocationThrottles, InputTokenCount, OutputTokenCount, with ModelId dimension, also check metrics under different ModelId forms like inference profile IDs 'us.' / 'global.'). The earlier list_metrics for InvocationThrottles in us-east-1 returned empty — double-check with other metric names/dimensions and say clearly if no throttling data exists in this account/region (it may be that calls come from another account or region, or cross-region profile throttling shows in a different region). 4) For the top models by usage, compute peak per-minute requests and tokens (note: Bedrock counts tokens with burndown, e.g. output tokens may be weighted 5x for some Claude models, and max_tokens reserved at request start counts toward TPM) and compare to the applied quota to show headroom. Return concise findings: a table of model, applicable quota name+code, applied value, default value, peak observed usage, throttle count; plus anything surprising."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review Bedrock quotas versus defaults and actual usage"}], "created_at": null}

