Instead, a more efficient path is checking the quota change history (which is small) alongside CloudWatch metrics for throttling and usage peaks on Bedrock models like Sonnet, then deciding afterward whether limited additional pagination is worthwhile.

"}], "parent_id": "71eadb7a-b576-46a7-8eb5-e001c442991f"}

{"id": "7b381be6-e85c-4403-91c4-a44b8f578115", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm noticing the peak values don't match between queries—the max I saw earlier was likely off because that query returned a limited, truncated set of points compared to this fuller result set.

The real peak looks like it's around 2.16M TPM, occurring near the end of the data on 11-23, roughly 20:2x UTC, with several other high peaks (1.5M, 1.29M, 1.27M, 1.05M) clustered on that same day. I also notice the dataset jumps from 11-20 straight to 11-23 19:52–20:47, which lines up with the earlier daily bucket showing 11-22 21:00 to 11-23 21:00. No InvocationThrottles data shows up, suggesting no recorded 429 throttling, though I still want to check ClientErrors per model before ruling that out. Next I need to page through quota information to check Sonnet 5's limits.

"}], "parent_id": "71eadb7a-b576-46a7-8eb5-e001c442991f"}

{"id": "4b412b6b-c506-43ef-93a6-8967330e4845", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Paging through quota listings this way seems impractical given the volume of pages and tokens involved, and I don't have reliable memory of the actual quota codes for Sonnet 4.5 cross-region throughput. I should consider a different approach rather than trying to recall or guess specific codes.

Given the small page sizes returned, covering the full set could take dozens of calls. Rather than keep paging blindly, I'll try a handful more targeted pages looking for "Sonnet 5" while also running a documentation search for typical default token-per-minute values for Claude Sonnet 5 cross-region quotas, since that could help me sanity-check whether the 2.16M TPM usage peak is notable.

"}], "parent_id": "71eadb7a-b576-46a7-8eb5-e001c442991f"}

{"id": "8d586987-87cd-4619-930f-f69ce07e074d", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This isn't converging on a clean count. I've gone through about six pages now, roughly 110 quotas, with page sizes varying quite a bit between 9 and 22 entries, so I'll push through maybe four more pages before settling for an estimate rather than chasing an exact total.

Rather than keep paging for Sonnet 5's actual limit, I'm thinking about checking CloudWatch for throttling signals instead — maybe model invocation logging is enabled and I can look for ThrottlingException entries in the logs. I'll check for a Bedrock log group under /aws/bedrock to see if that's a viable path.

"}], "parent_id": "71eadb7a-b576-46a7-8eb5-e001c442991f"}

{"id": "62fc036f-bbba-4623-873e-fb91aad0451e", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Now I have 667 points showing a max TPM near 2.16M, with five minutes exceeding 1M and 22 exceeding 500k; peak invocations hit 93/min, input 1.42M/min, output 109k/min. The burndown math checks out roughly - 1.42M plus five times 109k lands around 1.96M, close enough to the 2.16M estimate to account for max_tokens reservation, so I think I can stop digging into quota numbers here.

Without a direct service-quota lookup, I'll cross-check what I already found - Nova Lite cross-region RPM 4000, Haiku 4.5 cross-region RPM 10000, Nova 2 Lite global RPM 2000 - against their defaults, and also pull quota values for Llama 3 8B and the other listed quota codes.

"}], "parent_id": "71eadb7a-b576-46a7-8eb5-e001c442991f"}

{"id": "eb72f44a-79a3-41b5-83d4-58819c416f31", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Paging finished, but I realize my filters weren't consistent across pages—page 1 caught all RPM/TPM limits, while pages 2-6 only filtered by specific model names, so I likely missed Anthropic TPM entries for other models not covered in those later filters.

Interestingly, I found no RPM quota for Claude Sonnet 5 despite checking—it only has TPM, which is worth noting. Now I need to pull the default values for the remaining quota codes I haven't checked yet, running these lookups in parallel where possible.

"}], "parent_id": "685bd70a-a57c-45e5-9c87-7617c2ee698e"}

{"id": "7faed10c-859a-45c9-9def-eac1f33b67fb", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I need to correct myself here — Sonnet 5's quota is actually 6M TPM by default, so my earlier suggestion to request a quota increase wasn't warranted. The user's real limit is 6M TPM with no RPM cap, and their peak usage of 2.16M only represents 36% of that default allocation.

That 6M figure already accounts for estimated usage including max_tokens reservations, but CloudWatch only reports per-minute maximums, so throttling could still happen from sub-minute bursts that don't show up in the aggregated metrics. I should keep my answer concise.

"}]}

